    "question": {
      ":question/id": "37102589-15c6-5427-8585-0cbfb6bbebbe",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=3\\sin\\theta-2\n$$\n\n$$\ny=-3\\cos\\theta-2\n$$\n\n$$\n0\\le \\theta<\\frac{3\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y$? Use $<$ or $\\le$ in the comparison blanks.\n\nThe range of $x$ is {{field-1}} {{field-2}} $x$ {{field-3}} {{field-4}}.\n\nThe range of $y$ is {{field-5}} {{field-6}} $y$ {{field-7}} {{field-8}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Consider each coordinate separately.\n\nFor $x$:\n\nCheck the quarter-turn points in the parameter interval and its endpoint limits: $\\theta=0$ gives $\\sin\\theta=0$ (included); $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ gives $\\sin\\theta=1$ (included); $\\theta=\\pi$ gives $\\sin\\theta=0$ (included); $\\theta=\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$ gives $\\sin\\theta=-1$ (excluded endpoint).\n\nThe value $-1$ is approached at an excluded endpoint and is not attained elsewhere in the interval.\n\nThe value $1$ is attained at $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$.\n\nMultiply by $3$ and translate by $-2$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-1 &<\\sin\\theta\\le 1 \\\\\n-3 &<3\\sin\\theta\\le 3 \\\\\n-5 &<3\\sin\\theta-2\\le 1 \\\\\n-5 &<x\\le 1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $-5<x\\le 1$.\n\nFor $y$:\n\nCheck the quarter-turn points in the parameter interval and its endpoint limits: $\\theta=0$ gives $\\cos\\theta=1$ (included); $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ gives $\\cos\\theta=0$ (included); $\\theta=\\pi$ gives $\\cos\\theta=-1$ (included); $\\theta=\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$ gives $\\cos\\theta=0$ (excluded endpoint).\n\nThe value $-1$ is attained at $\\theta=\\pi$.\n\nThe value $1$ is attained at $\\theta=0$.\n\nMultiplying by $-3$ reverses both comparisons; translate by $-2$, then write the bounds in increasing order.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-1 &\\le \\cos\\theta\\le 1 \\\\\n3 &\\ge-3\\cos\\theta\\ge-3 \\\\\n1 &\\ge-3\\cos\\theta-2\\ge-5 \\\\\n-5 &\\le y\\le 1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $-5\\le y\\le 1$.\n\nContinuity gives every value between the stated bounds. The coordinate extrema need not occur at the same parameter value.",
